IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `scja-core`

-- scja2-part4-ad-appeals.l4: Part 4 (Appellate Division), Division 3, Matters that are non-appealable or appealable only with permission: ss 46 and 47.
-- Supreme Court of Judicature Act 1969 (Singapore), 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation in force from 1 July 2024.
-- The source is a plain-text rendering (SCJA1969.txt), not the official text. Layer 1: the law, objectively; every name begins with the label of its provision.
-- JUST TRUE and JUST FALSE are answers the Act gives in its own words; NOTHING means the Act does not answer on these facts (see NOTES.md, "Reading a result: FALSE and NOTHING (scja2)").

§ `Part 4, Division 3 -- Matters that are non-appealable or appealable only with permission`

§§ `s 46 -- No appeal in certain cases`

-- src:2812 | 46. An appeal cannot be brought against a decision of the Appellate
-- src:2813 | Division in the cases specified in the Ninth Schedule.
@ref s 46 (src:2812-2813)
GIVEN `sch 9 the Appellate Division decision is a case specified in the Ninth Schedule` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 46 an appeal can be brought against the decision of the Appellate Division` MEANS
    CONSIDER `sch 9 the Appellate Division decision is a case specified in the Ninth Schedule`
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "An appeal cannot be brought against a decision of the Appellate Division in the cases specified in the Ninth Schedule."
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST FALSE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the bar is for "the cases specified in the Ninth Schedule"; whether an appeal lies in any other case is for section 47
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN NOTHING
    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether the decision is a case specified in the Ninth Schedule is answered by another part of this encoding (PLAN.md seam 15)
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

§§ `s 47 -- Permission required to appeal`

-- src:2819 | 47.—(1) An appeal against a decision of the Appellate Division
-- src:2820 | made in the exercise of its appellate civil jurisdiction may only be
-- src:2821 | brought with the permission of the Court of Appeal.
@ref s 47(1) (src:2819-2821)
GIVEN decision IS A Decision
      `the Court of Appeal has given permission` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 47(1) an appeal against the decision may be brought` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the subsection is about "a decision of the Appellate Division made in the exercise of its appellate civil jurisdiction"
        IF NOT (decision's `made by` EQUALS `Appellate Division` AND decision's `in the exercise of` EQUALS `appellate jurisdiction` AND decision's `nature of the proceeding` EQUALS `a civil proceeding`) THEN NOTHING
        IF `the Court of Appeal has given permission` THEN JUST TRUE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "may only be brought with the permission of the Court of Appeal"
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

-- src:2827 | (2) The Court of Appeal may grant permission under subsection (1)
-- src:2828 | only if the appeal will raise a point of law of public importance.
@ref s 47(2) (src:2827-2828)
GIVEN `the appeal will raise a point of law of public importance` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 47(2) the Court of Appeal may grant permission under subsection (1)` MEANS
    IF `the appeal will raise a point of law of public importance`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "may grant permission under subsection (1) only if the appeal will raise a point of law of public importance"
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- src:2834 | (3) In deciding whether to grant permission under subsection (1) or
-- src:2835 | in determining, for the purposes of subsection (2), whether an appeal
-- src:2836 | will raise a point of law of public importance, the Court of Appeal is
-- src:2837 | to have regard to matters prescribed by the Rules of Court.
@ref s 47(3) (src:2834-2837)
GIVEN `the matter is one prescribed by the Rules of Court for this purpose` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 47(3) the Court of Appeal is to have regard to the matter` MEANS
    IF `the matter is one prescribed by the Rules of Court for this purpose`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the matters are those "prescribed by the Rules of Court", which are not among the sources; the text says nothing of a matter they do not prescribe
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:2850 | (a) the Court of Appeal is not required to grant permission
-- src:2851 | under subsection (1) even if the appeal will raise a point of
-- src:2852 | law of public importance; and
@ref s 47(4)(a) (src:2850-2852)
GIVEN `the appeal will raise a point of law of public importance` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 47(4)(a) the Court of Appeal is required to grant permission under subsection (1)` MEANS
    IF `the appeal will raise a point of law of public importance`
    THEN
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "the Court of Appeal is not required to grant permission under subsection (1) even if the appeal will raise a point of law of public importance"
        JUST FALSE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the paragraph speaks of an appeal that will raise a point of law of public importance; for another appeal subsection (2) applies
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:2856 | (b) permission may be granted under subsection (1) even if the
-- src:2857 | decision of the Appellate Division sought to be appealed
-- src:2858 | against —
-- src:2860 | (i) was made in an appeal transferred by the Court of
-- src:2861 | Appeal to the Appellate Division under
-- src:2862 | section 29E(1); or
-- src:2864 | (ii) was made in an appeal to the Appellate Division that
-- src:2865 | the Court of Appeal declined to transfer to itself
-- src:2866 | under section 29D(1).
@ref s 47(4)(b) (src:2856-2866)
GIVEN `s 29E(1) the appeal was transferred to the Appellate Division` IS A BOOLEAN
      `s 29D(1) the Court of Appeal declined to transfer the appeal to itself` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 47(4)(b) permission may be granted under subsection (1) despite the history of the appeal` MEANS
    IF `s 29E(1) the appeal was transferred to the Appellate Division` OR `s 29D(1) the Court of Appeal declined to transfer the appeal to itself`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "To avoid doubt, permission may be granted ... even if" the appeal was transferred under section 29E(1) or the Court of Appeal declined to transfer it under section 29D(1); the paragraph says nothing of another history
    ELSE NOTHING
